\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 1\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Physics-based Docking Flow Chart. The figure is an original synthesis drawn by the author from the workflows described in \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ref015}, \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ref017}, \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ref018}, \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ref019}, \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ref020} and \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ref021}, and is neither reproduced nor adapted from any single source}}{3}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 2\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Post-hoc optimisation and PoseBusters validity. Each tool is shown raw beside its optimised variants, with the dominant variant of each family being AutoDock Vina + gnina, DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina. The panel token \texttt {gnina-opt} denotes the gnina rescoring arm called AutoDock Vina + gnina throughout this thesis, and \texttt {gnina CNN-refine} denotes the separate gnina refinement arm, which is not the reported pipeline. Significance brackets carry Holm-corrected p-values, where *** p \textless {} 0.001, ** p \textless {} 0.01 and * p \textless {} 0.05}}{25}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 3\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Best-of-top-N accuracy against the as-placed crystal RMSD, for AutoDock Vina + gnina, DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina}}{36}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 4\normalsize }{\ignorespaces The same best-of-top-N accuracy against the best-fit (Kabsch) RMSD threshold, for the same three dominant variants}}{37}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 5\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Form versus in-place RMSD across ranking depth. Rows are the dominant variant of each family, namely AutoDock Vina + gnina, DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina}}{39}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 6\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Top-N crystal-cluster co-recovery. Each lower-triangular panel is one ranking depth, with the AutoDock arm labelled AutoDock Vina + gnina}}{44}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 7\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Composition and geometry of the crystal-closest cluster (n = 303, top-15 poses per tool, AutoDock arm labelled AutoDock Vina + gnina). Rates are taken over all 303 complexes, of which 24 contribute no pose to any panel, namely the 21 whose nearest cluster lies beyond the 4 \ensuremath {\text {\r A}}{} crystal-site threshold and so carry no recovered region at all, together with three further complexes whose qualifying cluster holds only a pose ranked outside the top-15 pool shown here. Panel A gives the per-tool pose contribution and reach, panel B the consensus richness, panel C the internal tightness of each tool\textquotesingle s own poses and panel D the spatial spread against consensus richness}}{45}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {\small 8\normalsize }{\ignorespaces Cluster-quality assessment (n = 303, 8 \ensuremath {\text {\r A}}{} centroid-clustering cut and 4 \ensuremath {\text {\r A}}{} crystal-site recovery threshold). Panels A-C show internal-validity indices, panel D compactness against separation, panel E bootstrap stability and panel F precision-at-one of five ranking rules against the oracle ceiling}}{47}{figure.caption.17}%
